      "id": 1,
      "text": "Coventry was officially named in 1711 and incorporated in 1712, making it one of Connecticut's earliest settlements.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.ct.one/towns/coventry",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The first house in Coventry, built by Nathaniel Rust near Wangumbaug Lake, dates back to the early 1700s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coventry%2C_Connecticut",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "South Coventry Village, listed on the National Register, houses Victorian homes, a museum, the public library, and Bidwell Tavern, opened in 1822.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coventry%2C_Connecticut",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Wellwood General Store, built in 1787, claims to be America’s oldest general store and now operates as the Coventry Country Store.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coventry%2C_Connecticut",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Nathan Hale Homestead, an operating museum, preserves the life of the patriot who lived there in the late 1700s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.coventry-ct.gov/index.aspx?NID=271",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Patriots Park on Wangumbaug Lake features a guarded beach, playground, lodge, community center, and a band shell for summer concerts.",
